{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_74","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in \"$TMPDIR/mu_detail_department-of-agriculture_m-sc-agro-forestry_.html\" \"$TMPDIR/mu_detail_department-of-agriculture_m-sc-forestry-medicinal-and-aromatic-technology_.html\" \"$TMPDIR/mu_detail_department-of-agriculture_m-sc-forestry-mushroom-technology_.html\"; do echo ===$(basename \"$f\"); python3 - \"$f\" <<'\"'PY'\nfrom html.parser import HTMLParser\nimport sys,re\nclass P(HTMLParser):\n def __init__(self):super().__init__();self.skip=0;self.lines=[];self.buf=[]\n def handle_starttag(self,t,a):\n  if t in ('script','style','noscript'):self.skip+=1\n  if t in ('h1','h2','h3','h4','h5','h6','p','li','td','th','div','section') and self.buf:self.flush()\n def handle_endtag(self,t):\n  if t in ('script','style','noscript') and self.skip:self.skip-=1\n  if t in ('h1','h2','h3','h4','h5','h6','p','li','td','th','div','section'):self.flush()\n def handle_data(self,d):\n  if not self.skip:\n   s=re.sub(r'\\\\s+',' ',d).strip()\n   if s:self.buf.append(s)\n def flush(self):\n  x=' '.join(self.buf).strip()\n  if x:self.lines.append(x)\n  self.buf=[]\np=P();p.feed(open(sys.argv[1],encoding='utf-8',errors='ignore').read())\nfor i,x in enumerate(p.lines):\n if 175<=i<=225: print(f'{i}: {x}')\nPY\ndone\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_74","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in \"$TMPDIR/mu_detail_department-of-agriculture_m-sc-agro-forestry_.html\" \"$TMPDIR/mu_detail_department-of-agriculture_m-sc-forestry-medicinal-and-aromatic-technology_.html\" \"$TMPDIR/mu_detail_department-of-agriculture_m-sc-forestry-mushroom-technology_.html\"; do echo ===$(basename \"$f\"); python3 - \"$f\" <<'\"'PY'\nfrom html.parser import HTMLParser\nimport sys,re\nclass P(HTMLParser):\n def __init__(self):super().__init__();self.skip=0;self.lines=[];self.buf=[]\n def handle_starttag(self,t,a):\n  if t in ('script','style','noscript'):self.skip+=1\n  if t in ('h1','h2','h3','h4','h5','h6','p','li','td','th','div','section') and self.buf:self.flush()\n def handle_endtag(self,t):\n  if t in ('script','style','noscript') and self.skip:self.skip-=1\n  if t in ('h1','h2','h3','h4','h5','h6','p','li','td','th','div','section'):self.flush()\n def handle_data(self,d):\n  if not self.skip:\n   s=re.sub(r'\\\\s+',' ',d).strip()\n   if s:self.buf.append(s)\n def flush(self):\n  x=' '.join(self.buf).strip()\n  if x:self.lines.append(x)\n  self.buf=[]\np=P();p.feed(open(sys.argv[1],encoding='utf-8',errors='ignore').read())\nfor i,x in enumerate(p.lines):\n if 175<=i<=225: print(f'{i}: {x}')\nPY\ndone\"","aggregated_output":"===mu_detail_department-of-agriculture_m-sc-agro-forestry_.html\n175: Free Legal Aid\n176: Public Self-Disclosure\n177: Refer a Friend\n178: Facebook X Instagram Linkedin YouTube\n179: Toggle Menu\n180: Duration\n181: 2 Years Programme\n182: Eligibility\n183: B.sc(H) Agriculture with 50% Marks.\n184: Fees\n185: INR Total 60,000/- Year INR Only\n186: About Programme\n187: Agriculture is changing—and so is our approach towards forests, land use, and sustainability. At Mangalayatan University, the Faculty of Agriculture aims at developing graduates that would strike a balance between being productive and environmentally responsible. The M.Sc. Forestry (Agro-Forestry) program at Mangalayatan University, Jabalpur, is meant for individuals aspiring to a career in sustainable land use, climate adaptation, and greener development.\n188: Indeed, today, agroforestry cannot be seen only as an innovative idea—it is an absolute need. According to the findings from international environmental research, agroforestry can help boost crop productivity by 30%, improve the quality of the soil, and support biodiversity. Moreover, there are ongoing efforts from the Indian government aimed at promoting agroforestry as a strategy to combat climate change, deforestation, and decreasing productivity.\n189: The importance of this field has resulted in a growing demand for specialists who are qualified in forestry and agroforestry. At Mangalayatan University, Jabalpur, the objective is to produce professionals who can contribute towards sustainable agriculture and conservation of the environment. The university focuses on not only theoretical knowledge but also practical exposure, field training, and problem-solving.\n190: There are experienced academicians, researchers, and industrial professionals who teach students about forestry and its applications in an environment-friendly manner. They are trained to combine the cultivation of trees with crops and livestock. The mode of instruction is not restricted to classroom teaching alone. Instead, students get involved in practical training, field work, nursery cultivation, planting exercises, and research-oriented projects.\n191: Modern laboratories, research institutions, and a healthy academic atmosphere make sure that holistic development takes place at Mangalayatan University. Critical thinking and decision-making abilities of the students enhance their environmental knowledge. Becoming a part of the M.Sc. Forestry (Agro-Forestry) course offered by Mangalayatan University at Jabalpur will help students shape their career in an influential way.\n192: Your Roadmap to Admission & Success\n193: Overview of the M.Sc. Forestry (Agro-Forestry) Program\n194: The M.Sc. Forestry (Agro-Forestry) course is regarded as one of the most relevant postgraduate courses in today’s eco-friendly era. With issues such as climate change, land degradation, and scarce resources becoming more common day by day, agroforestry has gained significant importance.\n195: As per reports published internationally, agroforestry holds the capability of restoring over 1 billion hectares of degraded lands globally, thus being one of the most effective environmental techniques.\n196: The M.Sc. Forestry (Agroforestry) course offered at Mangalayatan University is a two-year postgraduate course that aims to impart advanced learning in topics related to forestry and agroforestry. The M.Sc. Forestry (Agro-Forestry) course would suit candidates who are interested in nature, sustainability, and agriculture.\n197: What will you learn in this program?\n198: The curriculum is designed to combine scientific knowledge with practical application:\n199: Principles of Forestry and Agroforestry\n200: Tree Improvement and Nursery Management\n201: Soil Science and Land Management\n202: Agroforestry Systems and Practices\n203: Forest Ecology and Biodiversity\n204: Climate Change and Environmental Sustainability\n205: Watershed Management\n206: Forest Policy and Governance\n207: Remote Sensing and GIS in Forestry\n208: Research Methodology and Data Analysis\n209: The course begins with fundamental topics in forestry and progresses towards more advanced aspects of agroforestry and environmental management.\n210: Through hands-on experience with field work, research projects, and case studies, students learn to apply their knowledge to real-life situations.\n211: For those who wish to make a mark both professionally and in the field of environmental conservation, this course is ideal.\n212: Eligibility Criteria for the M.Sc. Forestry (Agro-Forestry) Program\n213: To apply for the M.Sc. in Forestry (Agro-Forestry) at Mangalayatan University, candidates must meet the following criteria:\n214: Graduates must have a bachelor’s degree in forestry, agriculture, horticulture, or a related field.\n215: They should have a minimum of 50% marks (relaxation as per university norms).\n216: They should be interested in environmental science, forestry, and sustainability.\n217: Admission Process for M.Sc. Forestry (Agro-Forestry) Program\n218: The admission process is simple and student-friendly:\n219: Fill out the application form (online/offline).\n220: Submit required academic documents.\n221: Appear for counseling (if required).\n222: Selection based on academic performance.\n223: Confirm admission through fee payment.\n224: Objectives of the M.Sc. Forestry (Agro-Forestry) Program\n225: The program is designed with clear and practical objectives:\n===mu_detail_department-of-agriculture_m-sc-forestry-medicinal-and-aromatic-technology_.html\n175: Free Legal Aid\n176: Public Self-Disclosure\n177: Refer a Friend\n178: Facebook X Instagram Linkedin YouTube\n179: Toggle Menu\n180: Duration\n181: 4 Years Programme\n182: Eligibility\n183: B.sc(H) Agriculture with 50% Marks.\n184: Fees\n185: INR Total 60,000/- Year INR Only\n186: About Programme\n187: In the current era, the significance of health, sustainability, and natural cures has gained tremendous value. The Faculty of Agriculture at Mangalayatan University aims at training skilled and knowledgeable graduates through the M.Sc. Forestry (Medicinal and Aromatic Technology) course to serve the purpose of blending traditional wisdom with modern scientific practice.\n188: For thousands of years, MAPs have played an important role in human civilization. Even in today’s era, research reveals that 80 percent of the people in developing nations depend on plant-based drugs for their primary health care. This shows the increasing importance of natural products in today’s health care system.\n189: India is among those countries that abound in biological diversity, having some 45,000 different plant species, of which about 3,500 can be used for their medical properties. Such huge potential results in growing scientific, agricultural, and commercial interest in medicinal and aromatic plants.\n190: Moreover, increased popularity of herbal medicines, cosmetics, and other organic products leads to rapid development of this market. The market of medicinal and aromatic plants is developing very fast in the context of growing demands for plant-based solutions.\n191: It is here that the M.Sc. Forestry (Medicinal and Aromatic Technology) course offered by Mangalayatan University finds its application. Mangalayatan University pays much attention to developing individuals with knowledge of both economic perspectives and ecology.\n192: The teaching faculty comprises academicians, researchers, and experts from the industry who train students in theoretical aspects as well as practical application. The training is very much hands-on and focuses on the field. The students engage in plantation activities, management of herb gardens, laboratory work, and research-based projects.\n193: Mangalayatan University offers state-of-the-art labs, research centers, and a green campus atmosphere, making it the perfect place to study. Selecting this course is equivalent to joining an industry that contributes to health care, the environment, and the economy at once.\n194: Your Roadmap to Admission & Success\n195: Overview of the M.Sc. Forestry (Medicinal and Aromatic Technology) Program\n196: The M.Sc. Forestry (Medicinal and Aromatic Technology) is an exclusive master’s degree course meant to fulfill the rising need for professionals in the areas of herb science, phyto-products, and sustainable agriculture.\n197: The significance of this discipline is on the rise across the globe. Studies reveal that the market segment related to medicinal and aromatic plants would witness a CAGR of nearly 7.6%. Thus, this would emerge as one of the most rapidly growing market segments in agroforestry systems.\n198: The reasons behind this growth include industries like pharmaceuticals, cosmetics, nutraceuticals, and wellness products. Mangalayatan University’s M.Sc. The Forestry Medicinal and Aromatic Technology is a two-year postgraduate course that aims to impart extensive knowledge and skills related to this discipline.\n199: What will you learn in this program?\n200: The curriculum is structured to combine science, sustainability, and business applications:\n201: Fundamentals of Forestry and Agroforestry\n202: Medicinal and Aromatic Plants Cultivation\n203: Plant Biotechnology and Tissue Culture\n204: Phytochemistry and Extraction Techniques\n205: Essential Oils and Value Addition\n206: Herbal Product Development\n207: Soil Science and Sustainable Land Use\n208: Forest Ecology and Biodiversity\n209: Climate Change and Environmental Management\n210: Research Methodology and Data Analysis\n211: Students first learn about the basics of forestry and plants before advancing to more complex subjects, including extraction, manufacturing, and commercialization of medicinal plants. The course will also teach students how to incorporate medicinal plants in an agroforestry production system, which will boost farm productivity and sustainability.\n212: It has been shown that through agroforestry-based medicinal plant farming, farms have benefited economically. It is suitable for students interested in nature, research, sustainability, and entrepreneurship.\n213: Advantages of M.Sc. Forestry (Medicinal and Aromatic Technology) Program\n214: M.Sc. Forestry (Medicinal and Aromatic Technology) is one of those programs that combine sustainability, science, and career benefits in a unique way to become extremely relevant in the modern world. Due to the rise in popularity of herbal products, research shows that about 80 percent of the world population uses plant medicines for basic health care needs, while the medicinal and aromatic plant sector grows steadily by an annual rate of 7-8 percent. This course gives students advanced knowledge on cultivating, processing, and adding value to medicinal plants. The career prospects of graduates are numerous; they can apply their skills in the fields of pharmaceutical, cosmetic, nutraceutical, and organic agricultural industries. The first major benefit is its interdisciplinary nature, which covers forestry, agriculture, biotechnology, and business aspects of this sphere. The second advantage lies in the practical component of education, since it involves laboratory practice, field studies, and research work. The final bonus is the opportunity for entrepreneurship and launching your own herbal business venture.\n215: Eligibility Criteria for the M.Sc. Forestry (Medicinal and Aromatic Technology) Program\n216: To apply for the M.Sc. Forestry Medicinal and Aromatic Technology admission at Mangalayatan University, candidates must meet the following criteria:\n217: Graduates must have a bachelor’s degree in forestry, agriculture, horticulture, botany, or a related field.\n218: Candidates should have a minimum of 50% marks (as per university norms).\n219: They should have interest in plant science, sustainability, and herbal technology.\n220: Admission Process for M.Sc. Forestry Program\n221: The admission process is simple and student-friendly:\n222: Fill out the application form (online/offline).\n223: Submit academic documents.\n224: Attend counseling sessions (if required).\n225: Selection based on academic performance.\n===mu_detail_department-of-agriculture_m-sc-forestry-mushroom-technology_.html\n175: Free Legal Aid\n176: Public Self-Disclosure\n177: Refer a Friend\n178: Facebook X Instagram Linkedin YouTube\n179: Toggle Menu\n180: Duration\n181: 4 Years Programme\n182: Eligibility\n183: B.sc(H) Agriculture with 50% Marks.\n184: Fees\n185: INR Total 60,000/- Year INR Only\n186: About Programme\n187: Agriculture has undergone tremendous changes in recent years and has diversified beyond the conventional scope of farming operations. In today’s scenario, mushroom farming stands out as one of the rapidly growing sectors with increasing importance because of the great nutrition potential, low capital investment, and huge market potential. Students from the Faculty of Agriculture at Mangalayatan University gain exposure to these new areas through the Master of Science in Forestry (Mushroom Technology).\n188: Mushroom farming is becoming the fastest-growing industry among many other agricultural activities. The global mushroom market is predicted to expand at a rate of 9% CAGR driven by growing consumer preference for nutritious food products. They are very rich in nutritional components, especially proteins, vitamins, antioxidants, and medicinal properties.\n189: In India, the mushroom business is growing rapidly owing to increased awareness regarding health and nutrition. Research studies have found out that mushroom farming can earn 5 to 10 times more than conventional agricultural practices per unit area. This is where the significance of an M.Sc. in Forestry (Mushroom Technology) offered by Mangalayatan University comes into play.\n190: The university’s objective is to train individuals in developing their skills in the field of sustainable agriculture, food security, and rural upliftment. In the program, students will be equipped with skills in mushroom cultivation, production management, and value addition. The department consists of highly qualified academicians and experts from the industry who impart knowledge to the students on theoretical as well as practical aspects of mushroom farming.\n191: Education is applied and field-based. Practical learning is gained via laboratory work, agricultural practices, and research projects. Other areas like post-harvest operations, packaging, and marketing are also taught. The presence of contemporary laboratories, controlled environments, and a conducive learning environment make Mangalayatan University the right choice for holistic development. Opting for this course program translates to choosing a career in agriculture, nutrition, and entrepreneurship.\n192: Your Roadmap to Admission & Success\n193: Overview of the M.Sc. Forestry (Mushroom Technology) Program\n194: This M.Sc. Forestry (Mushroom Technology) course is a professional postgraduate study designed specifically to cope with an emerging need for experts in mushroom cultivation and technology.\n195: Mushrooms have become popular as a source of health. Studies show that the use of mushrooms around the world has been on the rise over the last ten years due to the adoption of vegetarian and sustainable foods.\n196: At Mangalayatan University, the M.Sc. Mushroom Technology course is a two-year postgraduate program.\n197: What will you learn in this program?\n198: The curriculum is designed to cover all aspects of mushroom technology:\n199: Fundamentals of Forestry and Agroforestry\n200: Basics of Mushroom Biology and Cultivation\n201: Spawn Production Technology\n202: Mushroom Cultivation Techniques\n203: Pest and Disease Management\n204: Post-Harvest Technology\n205: Value Addition and Processing\n206: Soil Science and Sustainable Practices\n207: Marketing and Entrepreneurship\n208: Research Methodology and Data Analysis\n209: Students start from simple basics to progress into advanced processes of production and commercialization. Research indicates that growing mushrooms uses less area and water than other crops, which makes mushroom farming environmentally friendly. This course is suitable for all those interested in agriculture, research, and entrepreneurship.\n210: Advantages of Pursuing M.Sc. Forestry (Mushroom Technology)\n211: The choice of pursuing an M.Sc. in Forestry (Mushroom Technology) guarantees promising careers due to the growing and sustainable nature of this industry. With mushrooms gaining prominence because of their high protein content, low fat content, and plant base, the world mushroom market has shown a growth of more than 9% in terms of CAGR. In addition, mushrooms have also proved to be medically effective, which makes them useful for both the pharmaceutical and food sectors. Another benefit of taking up such a course is the ability to generate considerable profit from the cultivation of mushrooms, as mushroom farming has been found to provide higher yields per square meter compared to conventional agricultural practices. The course offers comprehensive training in the field of cultivation, processing, and marketing of mushrooms. It thus prepares one for employment in various fields related to the industry or enables one to become an entrepreneur in this field.\n212: Eligibility Criteria for the M.Sc. Forestry (Mushroom Technology) Program\n213: To apply for the M.Sc. Forestry Mushroom Technology at Mangalayatan University, candidates must meet the following criteria:\n214: Candidates must have a Bachelor’s degree in forestry, agriculture, horticulture, botany, or a related field.\n215: They should have a minimum of 50% marks (as per university norms).\n216: Interest in agriculture and mushroom cultivation.\n217: Admission Process for M.Sc. Forestry Program\n218: The admission process is simple and student-friendly:\n219: Fill out the application form (online/offline).\n220: Submit academic documents.\n221: Attend counseling sessions (if required).\n222: Selection based on academic performance.\n223: Confirm admission through fee payment.\n224: Objectives of the M.Sc. Forestry (Mushroom Technology) Program\n225: The program is designed with clear objectives:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_75","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'sqlite3 -readonly -separator $'\"'\\\\t' assets/data/career_path.db \\\"SELECT id,slug,parent_id,name,stream_id FROM career_nodes ORDER BY id;\\\" > \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/career_nodes.tsv\"; sqlite3 -readonly -separator $'\"'\\\\t' assets/data/career_path.db \\\"SELECT id,name FROM streams ORDER BY id;\\\" > \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/career_streams.tsv\"; python3 - \"$TMPDIR/career_nodes.tsv\" \"$TMPDIR/career_streams.tsv\" <<'\"'PY'\nimport sys\nnodes={}; streams={}\nfor line in open(sys.argv[1],encoding='utf8'):\n    id,slug,parent,name,stream=line.rstrip('\\\\n').split('\\\\t')\n    nodes[int(id)]={'slug':slug,'parent':int(parent) if parent else None,'name':name,'stream':int(stream)}\nfor line in open(sys.argv[2],encoding='utf8'):\n    id,name=line.rstrip('\\\\n').split('\\\\t'); streams[int(id)]=name\nids=[3,20,23,27,30,31,37,42,46,50,51,63,66,67,68,71,72,80,85,89,93,174,198,208,309,338,339,342,343,347]\nfor i in ids:\n    cur=i; arr=[]\n    while cur is not None:\n        arr.append(nodes[cur]['name']); cur=nodes[cur]['parent']\n    arr.reverse(); arr.insert(0,streams[nodes[i]['stream']])\n    print(i,nodes[i]['slug'],' > '.join(arr))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
